WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
When building a react native application on a high-end desktop machine,

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.000
it is easy to operate under a false sense of security.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.000
The hardware simply powers through inefficient code.

4
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.000
The reality of production is different.

5
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Your code will eventually execute on mid-range mobile hardware,

6
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
with a fraction of that desktop CPU and memory.

7
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.000
While the logical mental model of react components and hooks transfers from web to mobile,

8
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.000
the margin for waste evaporates.

9
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Unnecessary re-renders that are hidden by brute force desktop processing

10
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.000
will immediately choke a mobile device.

11
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:37.000
On mobile, a minor inefficiency manifests as a critical, visible failure point known as JANK.

12
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.000
This timeline illustrates the strict mathematical constraint of mobile performance,

13
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.000
the continuous hardware frame budget.

14
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.000
At a 60 Hertz refresh rate, the device has a 16.6 millisecond window to update the screen.

15
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.000
All JavaScript execution, layout calculation, and UI painting must completely finish within this window

16
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.000
to maintain a fluid 60 frames per second.

17
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Many mid-range devices now use 120 Hertz screens,

18
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.000
which cuts that processing budget in half to roughly 8.3 milliseconds.

19
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.000
If computation exceeds this window, the system drops the frame,

20
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.000
creating a visible stutter that breaks the illusion of motion.

21
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Mastering react native performance is about engineering your logic to guarantee execution under that 8 millisecond wire.

22
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Managing these constraints requires a breakdown of the underlying software architecture.

23
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.000
React native utilizes a three-thread model.

24
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:37.000
The first is the JS thread, where typescript, react renders, hooks, and logic execute.

25
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:44.000
The second is the UI thread, handling view rendering, touch events, and native animations independently of your JavaScript code.

26
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Finally, native module threads manage internal background work, like IO and networking.

27
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.000
The system is vulnerable when an active animation crosses into the JS thread on every frame.

28
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.000
If the JS thread stalls for 16 milliseconds, the frame drops.

29
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Libraries like react native reanimated solve this by moving animation workloads entirely to the UI thread,

30
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.000
making them immune to JavaScript slowdowns.

31
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:15.000
The goal of mobile architecture is isolating the UI thread to ensure it is never blocked by the heavy lifting occurring on the JS thread.

32
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Isolating these threads historically exposed a flaw in the legacy architecture, the asynchronous bridge.

33
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.000
The legacy bridge worked by queuing and serializing all cross-thread data into discrete packets.

34
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.000
This serialization created an asynchronous traffic jam that caused UI jank during complex interactions.

35
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:39.000
The new architecture in react native 0.76 removes this bridge in favor of the JavaScript interface, or JSI.

36
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:48.000
JSI enables JavaScript to invoke C++ methods directly and synchronously via memory pointers, which removes the need for data serialization.

37
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:56.000
This direct access also enables turbo modules, which load lazily on first access, rather than slowing down the initial application startup.

38
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Eliminating the serialization queue removes the primary structural barrier to achieving 120 Hertz performance.

39
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.000
The transition to synchronous memory access changes how the system handles the drawing of UI components on the screen.

40
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:16.000
This is handled by fabric, a rendering engine that computes the entire react view tree natively in C++.

41
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Because layout is computed in C++, the system can shift work between background threads and the main thread, depending on the immediate context.

42
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:34.000
By managing the view tree in C++, the system can hold multiple UI states in memory simultaneously, enabling the concurrent rendering features of react 18.

43
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:43.000
When a high priority interaction like a user tap occurs, the system can pause a heavy low priority render tree computation to process the touch event immediately.

44
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:52.000
While fabric optimizes the rendering pipeline, it cannot compensate for a strategy that triggers thousands of unnecessary component updates.

45
00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:00.000
The new architecture provides the infrastructure for speed, but developer disciplines still dictates what data enters that pipeline.

46
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
We can see these principles in practice, by looking at the most common source of frame drops, large scrolling lists.

47
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:15.000
The web concept of DOM virtualization is handled by the built-in flat list component, which only renders the items currently visible on screen.

48
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:26.000
Nesting a flat list inside a scroll view is a critical error. It prevents the system from calculating the list's height, forcing it to render every item at once and defeating virtualization.

49
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:33.000
Setting a strict window size ensures that items scrolled far away are unmounted, keeping the memory footprint low.

50
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.000
The most effective optimization for scrolling is the item layout property.

51
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:44.000
Providing fixed item heights upfront allows the system to bypass expensive layout measurement steps during the scroll.

52
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:52.000
Aggressive virtualization and pre-computation transform an endless memory drain into a strictly managed data window.

53
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:58.000
Even a perfectly virtualized list will stall if the components inside it re-render without cause.

54
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.000
Passing inline objects or arrays directly in JSX defeats reacts internal shallow comparison checks.

55
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:11.000
Since every render creates a new memory reference, it triggers a cascade of evaluations across the JS thread.

56
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:17.000
Use memoization hooks for derived data and callbacks to maintain reference stability across render cycles.

57
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Protecting the JS thread depends on stopping renders before the pipeline even evaluates them.

58
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.000
Finally, developers must systematically diagnose performance leaks in a real-world environment.

59
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Never trust a desktop simulator. Its processing power masks the native bottlenecks of mobile hardware.

60
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:38.000
Accurate measurement requires testing on physical, mid-range Android devices.

61
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:45.000
The on-device perf monitor is the first place to look, providing live tracking of JS and UI frames per second.

62
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:53.000
For deeper analysis, the react native dev tools allow for component profiling and network inspection directly from the dev menu.

63
00:05:53.000 --> 00:06:02.000
While the new architecture addresses historical bridge constraints, achieving 120 Hertz performance still relies on measuring ruthlessly and defending the frame budget.

